sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch05-15> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "When a character lost in a breed reappears after many generations, the cause is a tendency to produce that character transmitted in every generation which at last prevails under favourable conditions, not a sudden resemblance to a distant ancestor.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Long-lost characters reappear through a tendency transmitted each generation";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter V, “Laws of Variation”; section “Reversions to long-lost characters.”. Scope: domestic breeds and wild species"
}
